ta pretty soon and well  actually it's in beta right now but is  going to be expanding pretty soon and  they don't really have the exact plans  right now in terms of a roadmap for  release date and since you won't be  playing these games on a pc that you own  you'll be paying nvidia as a service to  play these games and those are  relationships they're still working on  so it's still very young but cloud  gaming is really young in general but  and it's in beta so it's not even  something that is necessarily gonna come  to fruition they might see this as  something that really isn't working out  but one thing that is working out is the  g-sync something that they're adding  into two monitors and graphics cards  that's going to improve the way that  games look how does that work exactly so  in  pass what you would you would have is a  graphics card would render frames and  the monitor would have a constant  refresh rate and the relationship of the  rendered frames coming from the card to  the monitor with some time result in  screen tearing or jittering what they're  doing is they're going to hardware  manufacturing saying look take our video  chip put it in your monitor that way the  GPU and the monitor can talk to each  other and they can sync up the frame  rate display so what you have is a  system that doesn't require vsync which  traditionally would tax your PC thus  reducing the graphical fidelity just so  you wouldn't have screens hearing but  now it's done without vsync at all  you'll have different frame rates say 40  and 60 that look as smooth on the same  monitor without having to change any  settings so this is something they're  offering to hardware manufacturers for a  similar price point that traditional  monitored ship manufacturers would offer  them and really it's kind of a win-win  as long as you're buying the video GPU  you may as well buy an Nvidia g-sync  monitor as well so long as the price  isn't too different and it sounds like  it won't be and the last thing that we  checked out here at the booth was a new  chipset for mobile and tablet devices  and it is making some really amazing  looking games or some amazing looking  demos at least right now I guess there  aren't some games running on it yeah so  for android mobile devices and Vidya is  sort of evolving there tiger line now to  sort of incorporate the kepler cuda  cores into mobile devices right now  they've got 192 cores which is  approaching the sort of low to mid to  your range of their graphics desktop  variants and it looks the graphics that  are getting are very good the one caveat  here is our working with OpenGL which is  a really popular and powerful API but  it's not as popular as DirectX what  you're going to see is perhaps people  taking mobile gaming a little bit more  seriously as the graphics are going to  be able to compete with platforms such  as gaming consoles and even gaming  laptops and as you can connect mobile  devices to TVs that's going to be even  more attractive for game developers and  so that about wraps it up for NVIDIA  here it's stay tuned to gamespot com for  all of your CS and other video game  related needs and be sure to follow us  on Twitter and Facebook to see all of  the updates as they happen in real time  thanks a lot  you
 

